        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein[cite: 39]. A fundamental common law doctrine is the CONCURRENCE OF MENS REA AND ACTUS REUS: the specific intent to commit a felony must exist AT THE PRECISE TIME OF THE BREAKING AND ENTRY[cite: 39]. If an actor breaks and enters a dwelling with mere trespassory intent (e.g., intending only to warm up or sleep), and only forms the intent to steal AFTER he is already inside the structure, common law burglary is NOT committed as a matter of law[cite: 39]. There is no retroactive concurrence[cite: 39]. Because Defendant formed the intent to steal after entry, burglary fails completely[cite: 39]. However, taking and carrying away the gold rings with intent to permanently deprive constitutes completed common law grand larceny (Option B)[cite: 39]. Options A and C are incorrect because the felonious intent did not concur with the breaking and entry, defeating burglary and attempted burglary[cite: 39]. Option D is incorrect because grand larceny is a completed felony that supersedes simple trespass[cite: 39]."
